Add a shortwave CO2 absorptance to `swr`, with `co2sww` in `radmod_nl` beside
`o3uvw`, `o3visw` and `h2osww`.

WHAT IT CHANGES, AND IT IS A NEW ABSORBER RATHER THAN A RE-WEIGHTING.
`radmod.f90` has NO shortwave CO2 term at all: `swr` carries ozone in band 1 and
water vapour in band 2 and nothing else, and CO2 appears only in `lwr`, from
Sasamori (1968). That is faithful to Lacis and Hansen (1974), who did not
parameterise the near-infrared CO2 bands either, so the absorber is missing
rather than wrong. A K dwarf puts about 1.5x the Sun's share of its flux into
those bands, which is what makes the omission worth fixing here and not on Earth.

This patch adds, in `swr` and in band 2 only:

  * a well-mixed CO2 column integral per layer, reduced to standard pressure by
    sigma*ps/p0 the same way the water vapour amount above it is and `lwr`'s own
    CO2 amount is, and expressed in atmos-cm through `lwr`'s own constants
    `zpv2pm`, `zrco2` and `zfco2` so the shortwave column and the longwave one
    are the same quantity;
  * a closed-form absorptance A(u) = a1 ln(1 + b1 u) + a2 ln(1 + b2 u), fitted to
    Howard, Burch and Williams (1956) band absorptions integrated against a
    5772 K spectrum, each band charged only with the fraction of its interval
    water vapour has left;
  * that absorptance applied at the three sites the water vapour term already
    uses -- the initial upward-beam total, the downward beam per layer, and the
    upward scattered beam per layer -- divided by `zsolar2` for exactly the
    reason Eq. 21 is, because it too is a fraction of TOTAL incident flux;
  * the resulting transmissivity ADDED into `ztb2` and `ztb2u` beside water
    vapour's, not multiplied into it. The H2O overlap is already removed inside
    the absorptance, band by band, so multiplying the two transmissions would
    remove it a second time.

IT IS A NO-OP UNTIL THE NAMELIST TURNS IT ON. `co2sww` defaults to 0.0, which
means the term is ABSENT and not that it is solar-weighted -- upstream has no
shortwave CO2, so zero is what reproduces upstream, and a rebuilt binary
reproduces every run that exists bit for bit. This matches how `h2osww` and
`ndustrad` already behave and is the property that lets this land in the same
rebuild as `exoplasim-3.4.2-h2o-shortwave-weight.patch` without confusing the
two. 1.0 is the solar-weighted term; the value for a given host is the ratio of
its CO2 absorptance to the Sun's.

THE 2.7 UM OVERLAP, WHICH IS A DECISION AND IS MADE HERE.
Yamamoto (1962) drops the 2.7 um CO2 band outright "because of overlapping by
the strong 2.7 um H2O band". It is KEPT here and charged instead with the
fraction of its interval water vapour leaves, which is the same correction every
other CO2 band gets and is available because the overlap is computed band by band
rather than argued about. Keeping a term that exists is what
`CLAUDE.md`'s physics-is-not-a-knob rule asks for, and dropping it is reported as
the low end of the bracket rather than hidden. The band is about a sixth of the
total and the check below passes either way, so the decision does not decide the
check.

THE CHECK THAT COULD HAVE FAILED. Run at EARTH's column, EARTH's gravity and
EARTH's mean insolation, the same integration has to land inside the 1.5 to
2.5 W/m2 that Earth's near-infrared CO2 solar absorption is measured at. It
does, with the 2.7 um band in and with it out. Nothing in the derivation was
tuned to that range.

Derivation, the fit and its residuals, and the numbers:
`exoplasim/scripts/shortwave_band_weights.py --verify` and
`exoplasim/notes/shortwave-co2.md`.

